{ Welcome to the Scotch gambit: you crack open the middle, let him keep a pawn for a while, and get your pieces out first. Start with the king pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Your king pawn takes a big step into the middle, and both your queen and your light bishop can come out now. [%cal Gf1c4] } (1. d4 { The queen pawn is just as good a start, but it leads to very different games. } 1... d5 2. c4 { That is the queen gambit, where a pawn is offered on the other wing. Our gambit starts with the king pawn. [%csl Bc4] }) (1. Nf3 { A flexible first move, and a perfectly good one. } 1... d5 2. d4 { A quiet queen pawn game. Today the king pawn goes first. [%csl Bd4] }) (1. c4 { The English opening: just as good, and played from the side of the board. } 1... e5 2. Nc3 { A slow, roomy game. The Scotch gambit needs your king pawn in the middle. [%csl Bc4] }) 1... e5 { He meets you head on with his own king pawn. Against c5 or e6 you would be in another opening. Bring your king knight out and hit that pawn. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { The knight hits his pawn on e5, and your king side is one step closer to castling. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Nc3 { Just as good a developing move, though this knight does not hit anything yet. } 2... Nf6 { Level, and calm: that is the Vienna, another opening. In ours the king knight goes first. [%csl Bc3] }) (2. Bc4 { The bishop aims at f7 straight away, a fine move. } 2... Nf6 3. d3 { Level, but now a pawn has to guard e4, and the game turns quiet. The knight to f3 hits his pawn first. [%cal Bd3e4] }) (2. d4 { Opening the middle at once is the centre game. } 2... exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 { Level, but his knight hits your queen, and she has to move a second time. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (2. f4 { The kings gambit: you offer your f pawn at once. } 2... exf4 3. Nf3 { About level, and very sharp, with a study of its own. The Scotch gambit keeps your f pawn at home. [%csl Rf4] }) 2... Nc6 { His knight guards the pawn; a knight to f6 or a pawn to d6 would be other openings. Now crack the middle open: your queen pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yd2d4] } 3. d4 { That is the Scotch: your pawn and your knight both hit e5, so he has to decide at once. [%cal Gd4e5,Gf3e5] } (3. Bb5 { The bishop to b5 is the Ruy Lopez, one of the best openings there is. } 3... a6 { About level, and a study of its own. Today your queen pawn breaks the middle open. [%csl Bb5] }) (3. Bc4 { The bishop to c4 is the Italian game, just as good. } 3... Bc5 { Level: bishop faces bishop, and the game stays calm. That is another study. [%csl Bc5] }) (3. Nc3 { The second knight comes out, fine and quiet. } 3... Nf6 { Level: four knights out and nothing happening yet. That is another opening. [%csl Bc3] }) 3... exd4 { He takes. Here is the surprise: do not take back yet. Bring your bishop to c4, aiming straight at f7. [%cal Yf1c4,Gc4f7] } 4. Bc4 { That is the Scotch gambit: you leave him the extra pawn for now and race your pieces out. If his bishop comes to c5 or b4, your c pawn joins the gambit. [%cal Gc4f7] } (4. Nxd4 { Taking back at once is the Scotch game, and it is just as good. } 4... Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 { Level. But you never gave a pawn away, so this is not the gambit. [%csl Bc6] }) (4. c3 { The c pawn offers a second pawn at once: another gambit, and just as good. } 4... dxc3 5. Nxc3 Bb4 { Level: a pawn down for fast development, and his bishop already pins your knight. It is a different road. [%cal Rb4e1] }) (4. Qxd4 { The queen takes back the pawn. Look again: his knight on c6 guards d4. [%cal Rc6d4] } 4... Nxd4 5. Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen, and your knight only got that knight back. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (4. Ng5 { The knight jumps at f7 before your bishop is there to help. } 4... Be7 5. Nf3 { Better for him: your knight went out and came back, he kept the pawn, and you lost two moves. [%csl Rf3] }) (4. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, nearly as good, but it blocks your queen's road to d4. } 4... Be7 { About level: he develops calmly and keeps the extra pawn, and your knight on d2 is in the way. [%csl Rd2] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight hits your king pawn: push it forward and kick the knight. Against a slow move instead, like the bishop to e7 or the pawn to h6, take the pawn back with your knight. [%cal Ye4e5] } 5. e5 { Your pawn kicks the knight off f6 and grabs space in the middle. If his queen steps to e7 instead, just castle: taking your pawn would walk into a pin by your rook. [%cal Ge5f6] } (5. O-O { Castling is just as good: it leads to a wild line full of forcing tricks. } 5... Nxe4 { Level, but you are two pawns down until your rook and bishop win them back. The push to e5 is simpler. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn back looks natural, but it leaves your e pawn without a guard. } 5... Nxe4 { About level, but you are a pawn down: his knight took your e pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Ng5 { The knight jumps at f7, but his d pawn shuts your bishop out first. } 5... d5 6. exd5 Qe7+ 7. Kf1 { About level, but his queen check sent your king to f1, and it can never castle now. [%csl Rf1] }) (5. c3 { The c pawn offers a second pawn, but your e pawn is left alone. } 5... Nxe4 6. Qb3 Qf6 { Slightly better for him: he grabbed your e pawn too, and his queen guards f7. You are two pawns down. [%csl Rf7] }) (5. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on d4 can still take. } 5... dxc3 6. bxc3 { Lost for you: his pawn took your knight, and your pawn took only a pawn back. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (5. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn back. Count the guards: his knight on c6 covers d4. [%cal Rc6d4] } 5... Nxd4 6. Nxd4 { Lost for you: the knight took your queen, and you won only that knight back. [%csl Rd4] }) (5. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight, but your e pawn is left unguarded. } 5... Be7 6. O-O Nxe4 7. Bxe7 Nxe7 { Slightly better for him: a pair of bishops came off, and he took your e pawn too. You are two pawns down. [%csl Re4] }) 5... d5 { He hits your bishop with his d pawn. Do not run back: pin his knight with the bishop to b5. [%cal Yc4b5,Rd5c4] } 6. Bb5 { Now his knight on c6 is pinned to his king, and his other knight is still attacked by your pawn, so it has to move. If it goes to d7, castle before you take the pawn back. [%cal Gb5e8,Ge5f6] } (6. exf6 { Your pawn takes the knight, but his pawn takes your bishop just as quickly. } 6... dxc4 { Much better for him: bishop for knight, but you are still a pawn down, and his queen can take your pawn on f6. [%cal Rd8f6] }) (6. exd6 { Taking en passant looks active, but it helps his bishop out. } 6... Bxd6 7. O-O O-O { Better for him: his bishop came out while taking back, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd6] }) (6. Bb3 { Stepping back to b3 keeps the bishop, but the pin was stronger. } 6... Ne4 7. O-O Bg4 { Slightly better for him: his knight sits on e4, his bishop pins your knight to your queen, and he keeps the pawn. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (6. Bd3 { Back to d3, where the bishop stands in front of your queen. } 6... Ne4 { About level, but his knight jumps to e4, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (6. Be2 { Running home to e2 keeps the bishop safe, but it gives him time. } 6... Ne4 7. O-O Bc5 { Slightly better for him: his knight and bishop are out, and the pawn is still his. [%csl Rc5] }) (6. Bxd5 { The bishop takes the pawn, but his knight guards d5. [%cal Rf6d5] } 6... Nxd5 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and you got one pawn for it. [%csl Rd5] }) 6... Ne4 { The knight hops to e4, where your pawn cannot touch it. Time to collect: your knight takes the pawn on d4. [%cal Yf3d4] } 7. Nxd4 { The pawn is back, so material is level again, and your knight stands in the middle of the board. [%csl Gd4] } (7. O-O { Castling first is just as good. } 7... Bc5 8. Nxd4 O-O { Level: you castled first, and the pawn came back anyway. [%csl Gd4] }) (7. Bxc6+ { Taking the knight now comes a move too early: wait until he has spent a move breaking the pin. } 7... bxc6 8. Nxd4 { Slightly better for him: the pawn is back, but it is his move, and his light bishop is still free to come out. [%csl Rc8] }) (7. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn back, but out in the open she gets chased. } 7... Bc5 8. Qa4 Bxf2+ 9. Ke2 { Much better for him: his bishop took the pawn on f2 with check, and your king is stuck in the middle. [%csl Re2] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on d4 can take it. } 7... dxc3 8. bxc3 { Lost for you: a whole knight gone for one pawn. The pawn on d4 was still alive. [%csl Rc3] }) 7... Bd7 { His bishop breaks the pin, and now his knight on c6 can jump at your knight. Take it first: bishop takes knight. [%cal Yb5c6,Rc6d4] } 8. Bxc6 { Your bishop takes the knight, so it can never jump at your knight on d4. If his bishop takes back, your knight takes it, and his c pawns end up doubled anyway. [%cal Gb5c6] } (8. O-O { Castling is natural, but it gives his knight on c6 time to jump. } 8... Nxd4 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 10. Qxd4 { Level: a pair of knights and a pair of bishops came off, and his knight on e4 is still the proudest piece. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Nxc6 { Taking with the knight instead doubles his pawns too. } 8... bxc6 9. Bd3 Qe7 { Level, but your bishop was pushed back, and his queen already hits your e5 pawn. [%cal Re7e5] }) 8... bxc6 { He takes back with the b pawn, so now he has two pawns on the c column. Castle, and your king is safe. [%csl Rc6,Rc7][%cal Ye1g1] } 9. O-O { Your king is tucked away on g1. If his bishop goes to e7 now, your f pawn still comes to chase his knight. [%csl Gg1] } (9. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, just as good. } 9... Be7 10. O-O O-O { Level: your king castles a move later, with nothing lost. [%csl Gg1] }) (9. Nd2 { The knight comes out to hit his knight on e4. } 9... Qg5 { Level, but his queen jumps out and hits your g2 pawn while your king is still in the middle. [%cal Rg5g2] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out and hits his knight on e4, a natural move. } 9... Qe7 { About level, but your king still waits in the middle, and his queen already hits e5. [%cal Re7e5] }) 9... Bc5 { His bishop hits your knight on d4, and his knight on e4 looks proud. Count its safe squares, then kick it with the f pawn. [%cal Yf2f3,Rc5d4] } 10. f3 { The pawn hits the knight, and it has just one safe square left, g5. If he castled now, your pawn would take the knight and win a piece. [%cal Gf3e4] } (10. Be3 { Guarding the knight with the bishop is just as good. } 10... Bb6 11. Nd2 { Level: your knight on d4 is guarded, and your other knight now challenges his. [%cal Gd2e4] }) (10. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, also just as good. } 10... Bb6 11. Nd2 { Level: your knight is safe, and his proud knight on e4 now faces a challenger. [%csl Ge4] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out to trade off his strong knight, just as good. } 10... Nxc3 11. bxc3 { Level: his proud knight is gone, but your pawns on the c column are doubled. [%csl Rc3] }) (10. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but f2 is where his knight wants to jump. } 10... Nxf2 11. Rxf2 Bxd4 { Much better for him: he won the pawn on f2, and his bishop now pins your rook to your king. [%cal Rd4g1] }) 10... Ng5 { The knight runs to g5. Chase it once more: the f pawn takes another step. [%cal Yf3f4] } 11. f4 { Your pawn hits the knight again. If it jumps to e6, your knight on d4 is pinned to your king and attacked twice: guard it at once. [%cal Gf4g5] } (11. Be3 { The bishop guards your knight first, just as good. } 11... Bb6 { Level: your knight is guarded, and his knight on g5 still has few squares. [%csl Rg5] }) (11. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, also fine. } 11... O-O { Level: your knight is safe, and his king has castled. [%csl Gc3] }) (11. Nc3 { The queen knight comes out, just as good. } 11... Ne6 { Level: his knight hops to e6 and hits your knight on d4. [%cal Re6d4] }) (11. Bxg5 { Taking the knight gives up your dark bishop. } 11... Qxg5 { About level, but his two bishops now face your two knights, and his queen is in play. [%csl Rg5] }) (11. Re1 { The rook takes the e column, nearly as good. } 11... Ne6 { Level: his knight jumps to e6, and your f pawn never got to chase it further. [%csl Re6] }) 11... Ne4 { The knight hops back to e4. Bring out your last bishop, and it guards your knight on d4 too. [%cal Yc1e3,Rc5d4] } 12. Be3 { That is the Scotch gambit played through: the pawn came back, your king is castled, and your pawns on e5 and f4 cramp him. His c pawns are doubled. [%csl Ge5,Gf4,Rc6,Rc7] } (12. Nc3 { The knight comes out to trade off his best piece, just as good. } 12... Nxc3 13. bxc3 { Level: you traded off his proud knight, though now your c pawns are doubled too. [%csl Rc3] }) (12. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, also good. } 12... Bb6 { Level: your knight is safe on d4, and his bishop steps back to b6. [%csl Gd4] }) (12. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but your knight on d4 has lost a guard. } 12... Bxd4+ 13. Kh1 Nf2+ 14. Rxf2 Bxf2 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight with check, and his knight fork then cost you your rook. [%csl Rf2] }) (12. Re1 { The rook comes to the e column, but it does not guard your knight on d4. } 12... Qh4 13. Be3 Bxd4 14. Bxd4 { About level: the pieces came off on d4, and his queen now hits your pawn on f4. [%cal Rh4f4] }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: his knight jumps to g4"]
