{ Back in the Scotch gambit: your pawn on e5 has just kicked his knight. Where will it go? }
5... Ng4 { His knight jumps to g4, and two knights now hit your pawn. The check on f7 looks juicy. Is it the best you have? [%csl Re5] } 6. O-O { Castle: your king is safe, and grabbing your e pawn now would cost him. If his pawn comes to d6, take it. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 grabs a pawn and chases his king. } 6... Kxf7 7. Ng5+ Kg8 { Slightly better for him: his king hides on g8, and you gave a bishop for a pawn, though his knight on g4 is loose. [%csl Rg8] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen guards your pawn, a fair try. } 6... Qe7 7. Bf4 f6 { Level, but his f pawn now hits your pawn on e5 as well, and you are still a pawn down. [%cal Rf6e5] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop guards your pawn, but it can be hit. } 6... d6 7. exd6 Bxd6 { Slightly better for him: his bishop takes back and challenges yours, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%cal Rd6f4] }) (6. Nxd4 { Taking the gambit pawn back leaves your e pawn without its guard. } 6... Ngxe5 { Slightly better for him: his knight took your e pawn and now hits your bishop. [%cal Re5c4] }) (6. Nc3 { Developing the knight walks into the pawn on d4. } 6... dxc3 { Lost for you: the pawn took your knight, and winning that pawn back will not make up for it. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. Qxd4 { Grabbing the pawn with the queen runs into his knight on c6. [%cal Rc6d4] } 6... Nxd4 7. Nxd4 { Lost for you: a queen for a knight is a terrible trade. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but he blocks it easily. } 6... Be7 { Slightly better for him: his bishop blocks, and your pawn on e5 still hangs. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, where his pawn on d4 can take it. } 6... dxe3 7. fxe3 { Lost for you: a bishop gone for one pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (6. c3 { The c pawn offers a second pawn, but your e pawn needs help now. } 6... Ngxe5 7. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Better for him: he took your e pawn, and his knight on e5 hits your bishop. You are two pawns down. [%cal Re5c4] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but your e pawn still has only one guard. } 6... Ngxe5 { Better for him: his knight grabbed your e pawn, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Bd2 { The bishop steps to d2, but it does nothing for your e pawn. } 6... Ngxe5 { Better for him: the pawn on e5 fell, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Re5] }) 6... Be7 { His bishop comes out quietly; on c5 it would have been a little worse for him. Two knights still hit your pawn on e5, and only your knight guards it. What now? [%csl Re5] } 7. Re1 { The rook guards your pawn from behind. If his pawn comes to d5 to hit your bishop, pin his knight with the bishop to b5. [%cal Ge1e5] } (7. Bf4 { The bishop guards your pawn too, and it is just as good. } 7... g5 8. Bg3 { Level, but his g pawn chases your bishop, and the game turns wild. The rook guard is calmer. [%csl Rg5] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen guards the pawn, but his d pawn can swap it off. } 7... d6 8. exd6 Qxd6 { About level, but your pawn on e5 is gone, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. Nxd4 { Grabbing the gambit pawn back leaves your e pawn with no guard at all. } 7... Ngxe5 { Slightly better for him: your e pawn fell, and his knight lands on e5 with a threat. [%cal Re5c4] }) (7. h3 { Kicking the knight now comes too early: your pawn on e5 falls first. } 7... Ngxe5 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Better for him: your e pawn fell before the knight could be chased, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Re5] }) (7. c3 { The c pawn gives up a second pawn, and your e pawn still has only one guard. } 7... Ncxe5 { Better for him: his knight took your e pawn, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it lands where the pawn on d4 can take it. } 7... dxc3 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and taking back only wins a pawn. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (7. Qxd4 { The queen takes the gambit pawn, within reach of his knight on c6. [%cal Rc6d4] } 7... Nxd4 8. Nxd4 { Lost for you: you gave your queen and got only his knight. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Bg5 { The bishop pokes at his bishop, but the trade on g5 goes his way. } 7... Bxg5 8. Nxg5 Qxg5 { Lost for you: after the trades on g5 his queen collected your knight too. [%csl Rg5] }) (7. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it does not guard your pawn. } 7... Ngxe5 { Better for him: his knight grabbed your e pawn, and your knight on d2 did nothing to save it. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Be3 { The bishop steps out, straight into the path of his pawn on d4. } 7... dxe3 { Lost for you: his pawn took your bishop, and taking back only wins a pawn. [%cal Rd4e3] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 grabs a pawn, but your bishop is lost. } 7... Kxf7 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and a pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rf7] }) 7... O-O { He castles, and that is a mistake: his pawn to d6 had to come first. How do you punish it? } 8. h3 { The pawn kicks the knight, and it has no good square: on e5 your rook would win it. If it grabs the pawn on f2, take it with your king. [%cal Gh3g4] } (8. c3 { The c pawn offers another pawn, but his knight gets time to breathe. } 8... d5 9. exd6 Qxd6 { Level: his d pawn freed his game, and the chance to chase the knight is gone. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Bd5 { The bishop hits his knight on c6, but he simply hits back in the middle. } 8... d6 9. exd6 Bxd6 { Level: his pieces came out, and his knight on g4 is safe again. [%csl Rg4] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it lets his d pawn in. } 8... d5 { About level: his d pawn hits your bishop, and his knight on g4 stays put. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (8. Bb5 { The bishop hits his knight on c6, but his f pawn strikes first. } 8... f6 9. exf6 Bxf6 { About level: his pieces came alive, and your pawn on e5 is gone. [%csl Rf6] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop guards your pawn, but it can be traded off. } 8... d6 9. exd6 Bxd6 { Slightly better for him: his bishop challenges yours, and he is still a pawn up. [%cal Rd6f4] }) (8. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn back takes a guard away from your e pawn. } 8... Ncxe5 { Better for him: his knight took your e pawn and hits your bishop on c4. [%cal Re5c4] }) (8. Qe2 { The queen guards the pawn, but his d pawn breaks in anyway. } 8... d6 9. exd6 Bxd6 { Better for him: your e pawn is gone, and his bishop aims at your king side. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on d4 is still waiting. } 8... dxc3 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and you are a piece down. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (8. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but his rook guards that pawn. } 8... Rxf7 { Much better for him: his rook took your bishop, and you are a whole piece down. [%csl Rf7] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop offers a trade on g5, but you cannot take back safely. } 8... Bxg5 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, and his queen now guards g5, so taking back costs your knight. [%cal Rd8g5] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, where his d pawn can take it. } 8... dxe3 { Lost for you: his d pawn swallowed your bishop. [%csl Re3] }) (8. Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn on d4, but his knight on c6 guards it. [%cal Rc6d4] } 8... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen. [%csl Rd4] }) 8... Nh6 { The knight backs off to h6, on the edge of the board. What now? } 9. Bxh6 { Your bishop takes the knight, and only his g pawn can take back, which breaks the pawns in front of his king. [%csl Rg7] } (9. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn first is good too, but his knight escapes. } 9... Nxd4 10. Qxd4 Nf5 { About level: your pawn is back, but his knight got out through f5, and his pawns are whole. [%csl Gf5] }) (9. Bd3 { The bishop aims at his king side, but it lets the knight on h6 live. } 9... Kh8 { About level: his king steps into the corner, and the pawns in front of it are still whole. [%csl Gh8] }) (9. c3 { The c pawn offers a second pawn while his knight sits on h6. } 9... dxc3 10. Nxc3 Nf5 { About level: his knight found f5, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Rf5] }) (9. Qd2 { The queen lines up on h6, but his d pawn breaks first. } 9... d6 { About level: his d pawn hits yours, and the knight on h6 can still get out. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on d4 takes it. } 9... dxc3 { Much better for him: a knight lost for one pawn, and his knight on h6 is still alive. [%csl Rc3] }) (9. Bf4 { The bishop guards your pawn, but it lets the knight out. } 9... Nf5 { Level: his knight escaped to f5, and the chance to break his king side is gone. [%csl Rf5] }) (9. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it lets his knight escape. } 9... Nf5 { Level: his knight jumps to f5, and his king side stays whole. [%csl Rf5] }) (9. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7 and throws itself away. } 9... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rf7] }) (9. Be3 { The bishop steps into the path of his d pawn. } 9... dxe3 { Lost for you: his pawn took your bishop, a piece for a pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (9. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn on d4, forgetting his knight on c6. } 9... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) 9... gxh6 { He takes back with the g pawn, and his pawns on h6 and h7 are doubled. What now? } 10. Nxd4 { The gambit pawn is back, so material is level, and your knight eyes his knight on c6. If his d pawn hits your bishop, take it en passant. [%cal Gd4c6] } (10. c3 { The c pawn is just as good: the pawn on d4 falls anyway. } 10... d5 { Slightly better for you: your c pawn hits d4, and his broken king side stays broken. [%cal Gc3d4] }) (10. Bd5 { The bishop jumps to the middle, a fine move too. } 10... Kh8 { Slightly better for you: his king side is still broken, though the gambit pawn is still his. [%csl Rh6] }) (10. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, also good. } 10... Kh8 { Slightly better for you: his pawns are broken, but he keeps the extra pawn for now. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Qd2 { The queen goes after the pawn on h6, but that is too slow. } 10... d5 { Level: his d pawn strikes at your bishop, and the gambit pawn is still his. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the d4 pawn is still there to take it. } 10... dxc3 11. bxc3 { Lost for you: a whole knight for one pawn. [%csl Rc3] }) (10. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 costs your bishop. } 10... Rxf7 { Much better for him: his rook took the bishop, and your attack is over. [%csl Rf7] }) (10. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn, but his knight guards it. } 10... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen, and a knight is all you can win back. [%csl Rd4] }) 10... Nxd4 { He trades knights on d4. Take it back. } 11. Qxd4 { That is the Scotch gambit paying off: the pawn is back, your queen rules the middle, and his king hides behind broken pawns on h6 and h7. [%csl Gd4,Rh6,Rh7] } (11. Qg4+ { The queen check first is just as good. } 11... Kh8 12. Qxd4 { Better for you: the check drove his king to the corner, and your queen still took back on d4. [%csl Gd4] }) (11. Nc3 { Developing first forgets that his knight on d4 must be taken. } 11... Ne6 { Much better for him: his knight got away, and you are a piece down. [%csl Re6] }) 1-0


