{ The Scotch gambit, with his knight on f6 just kicked by your pawn. It has more than one square to choose from. }
5... Ne4 { The knight jumps forward to e4, into the middle, where your pawn cannot touch it. Grabbing the gambit pawn back looks tempting. Is it safe? [%csl Re4] } 6. O-O { Castle first: your king is safe, and the gambit pawn can wait. Against the bishop to e7, bring your rook to e1, and the game soon joins this line. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Qe2 { The queen hits his knight, just as good. } 6... Nc5 { Level: his knight backs off to c5, and your king is ready to castle. [%csl Gc5] }) (6. Bd5 { The bishop hits his knight from the middle, also fine. } 6... Nc5 { Level: his knight retreats to c5, and your bishop stands proud on d5. [%csl Gd5] }) (6. Nxd4 { Grabbing the pawn back leaves your e pawn unguarded. } 6... Nxe5 { Better for him: his other knight grabbed your pawn on e5 and hits your bishop. [%cal Re5c4] }) (6. c3 { The c pawn offers a second pawn, more than you need. } 6... d5 7. exd6 Nxd6 { About level: your pawn on e5 is gone, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rd6] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight comes out to trade off his knight. } 6... d5 { About level: his d pawn chases your bishop, and his knight holds on to e4. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop guards your pawn, but he can swap it off. } 6... d5 7. exd6 Bxd6 { Better for him: bishop meets bishop, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%cal Rd6f4] }) (6. Nc3 { Developing the knight puts it where the pawn on d4 can take it. } 6... dxc3 { Much better for him: that knight is lost for a pawn. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. Qxd4 { Grabbing the pawn with your queen ignores his knight on c6. [%cal Rc6d4] } 6... Nxd4 7. Nxd4 { Lost for you: the queen is gone, and a knight does not pay for her. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but his knight on e4 can take it. } 6... Nxg5 7. Nxg5 Qxg5 { Lost for you: both of your pieces on g5 were taken. [%csl Rg5] }) (6. Bd2 { The bishop steps to d2, but that gives his d pawn time. } 6... d5 { Better for him: his d pawn hits your bishop, and his knight on e4 stands firm. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 grabs a pawn, but his king takes your bishop. } 6... Kxf7 { Much better for him: a bishop for one pawn, and his knight still sits on e4. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. Be3 { The bishop comes to e3, where the pawn on d4 can take it. } 6... d5 { Lost for you: his d pawn hits your bishop, and his other pawn hits e3, two pieces at once. [%cal Rd5c4,Rd4e3] }) 6... d5 { He hits your bishop with his d pawn; with his bishop on c5 instead, his knight on e4 would have had no way home. Simply running away with your bishop loses time. [%cal Rd5c4] } 7. exd6 { Your pawn takes en passant, so his d pawn is gone and the e column opens. He can take back on d6 with three different pieces. [%csl Gd6] } (7. Bb5 { Pinning the knight with the bishop to b5 is just as good. } 7... Bc5 8. Nxd4 O-O { Level: the gambit pawn came back that way too. [%csl Gd4] }) (7. Bb3 { Stepping back to b3 lets his bishop pin your knight. } 7... Bg4 { Slightly better for him: the bishop on g4 pins your knight, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (7. Bd3 { Back to d3, where the bishop blocks your queen's view of d4. } 7... Bg4 { About level: he develops with a pin on your knight and keeps his extra pawn. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (7. Be2 { Stepping all the way back to e2 is too slow. } 7... Bc5 { Slightly better for him: your bishop hides on e2 while his pieces fill the middle. [%csl Rc5] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen hits his knight, but your bishop is still attacked by his pawn. } 7... dxc4 8. Qxe4 { Better for him: you won his knight back, but he got your bishop for it and keeps the pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Bxd5 { The bishop takes the pawn, but his queen guards d5. [%cal Rd8d5] } 7... Qxd5 { Lost for you: a whole bishop for one pawn, and his queen stands in the middle. [%csl Rd5] }) (7. Nxd4 { Taking the gambit pawn forgets your bishop on c4. } 7... dxc4 8. Nxc6 Qxd1 9. Rxd1 bxc6 { Lost for you: after the trades you are a whole piece down. Your bishop on c4 was hanging all along. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it does nothing for your bishop. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 dxc4 { Lost for you: you traded knights, then his pawn took your bishop. [%csl Rc4] }) 7... Nxd6 { He takes back with the knight, the best way, and it hits your bishop. What now? } 8. Re1+ { Check! His king has to deal with your rook first. Had his queen or bishop taken on d6, your rook would pin his knight on e4 instead. [%cal Ge1e8] } (8. Bd5 { The bishop jumps to the middle, just as good. } 8... Nf5 { Level: his knight hops to f5 to guard the pawn on d4. [%cal Rf5d4] }) (8. Bb3 { Stepping back to b3 is also fine here. } 8... Bg4 { Level: his bishop pins your knight, but your king is castled and his is not. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (8. Bd3 { Back to d3, in front of your queen. } 8... Be7 { About level, but your bishop blocks your queen, and the gambit pawn is still his. [%csl Rd3] }) (8. Be2 { The bishop runs home to e2. } 8... Nf5 { About level: his knight guards the pawn on d4 from f5, and your bishop sits back. [%cal Rf5d4] }) (8. Qe2+ { The queen gives the check instead, but then she stands in the way. } 8... Be7 { Slightly better for him: his bishop blocks, and your queen now stands where your rook wanted to go. [%csl Re2] }) (8. Nxd4 { Taking the gambit pawn forgets that your bishop on c4 is attacked. } 8... Nxc4 { Much better for him: his knight took your bishop for free. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but a pawn chases it off. } 8... f6 { Better for him: his pawn hits your bishop on g5 while his knight hits the one on c4, and you cannot save both. [%csl Rg5,Rc4] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, into the reach of the pawn on d4. } 8... dxc3 { Lost for you: the pawn took your knight, and your bishop on c4 still hangs. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (8. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but his knight simply takes it. } 8... Nxf7 { Lost for you: a bishop gone for one pawn, and his king is safe. [%csl Rf7] }) 8... Be7 { He blocks with the bishop. Your bishop on c4 is still attacked by his knight. Where does it belong? } 9. Bd5 { The bishop takes the middle of the board. His knight could jump to f5 or b4 next. [%csl Gd5] } (9. Bb3 { Stepping back to b3 is just as good. } 9... O-O 10. Nxd4 { Level: your knight takes back the gambit pawn at once. [%csl Gd4] }) (9. Bd3 { Back to d3, but there it just blocks your queen. } 9... O-O { About level, but your bishop does little on d3, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd3] }) (9. Be2 { The bishop steps back to e2. } 9... Nf5 { About level: your bishop hides on e2, and his knight on f5 keeps the gambit pawn safe. [%csl Rf5] }) (9. Bf1 { All the way back to f1 is safe but slow. } 9... Nf5 { About level: he keeps his extra pawn, and your bishop is back where it started. [%csl Rf1] }) (9. Nxd4 { The knight grabs the pawn, but your bishop on c4 is still attacked. } 9... Nxc4 { Lost for you: you grabbed a pawn and dropped a bishop. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Bg5 { The bishop offers a trade, but your other bishop is hanging. } 9... Nxc4 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop on c4. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Bb5 { The bishop pins his knight, but his knight on d6 guards b5. [%cal Rd6b5] } 9... Nxb5 { Lost for you: his knight simply took your bishop. [%csl Rb5] }) (9. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but your bishop on c4 is still hit. } 9... Nxc4 10. Nxc4 { Better for him: he traded his knight for your bishop and kept the gambit pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Bxf7+ { The bishop gives itself up on f7 with check. } 9... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took the bishop, and his king is safe behind it. [%csl Rf7] }) 9... O-O { He castles; his bishop to g4 or d7 were the other ideas. What now? } 10. Bxc6 { Your bishop removes the guard of the pawn on d4, and only his b pawn can take back. This trade also answers his bishop moves; against the knight jump to b4, step back first. [%cal Gd5c6][%csl Gd4] } (10. Nxd4 { Grabbing the pawn at once lets him trade knights. } 10... Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Bf6 { About level: the pawn is back, but his bishop hits your queen, and he has two bishops. [%cal Rf6d4] }) (10. c3 { The c pawn hits d4, but his bishop can pin your knight first. } 10... Bg4 { About level: his bishop pins your knight to your queen, and the pawn on d4 holds. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (10. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it does nothing for the fight over d4. } 10... Bf6 { About level: his bishop guards d4, and the gambit pawn is still his. [%cal Rf6d4] }) (10. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but your bishop on d5 can be kicked. } 10... Nb4 { Slightly better for him: his knight hits your bishop, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%cal Rb4d5] }) (10. h3 { A quiet pawn move gives his knight time. } 10... Nb4 { Slightly better for him: his knight kicks your bishop, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Rb4] }) (10. Qxd4 { The queen takes on d4, but the knight on c6 still guards it. [%cal Rc6d4] } 10... Nxd4 { Lost for you: your queen is gone for a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but his knight guards that square. } 10... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and his king is safe. [%csl Rf7] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out to c3, into the path of his pawn. } 10... dxc3 { Lost for you: his pawn took your knight. [%csl Rc3] }) (10. Be3 { The bishop comes to e3, where his pawn can take it. } 10... dxe3 { Lost for you: the pawn on e3 is his, and your bishop is gone. [%csl Re3] }) 10... bxc6 { He takes back with the b pawn. What now? } 11. Nxd4 { The Scotch gambit pawn is back, so material is level. His c pawns are doubled, and his a pawn stands alone. [%csl Gd4,Rc6,Rc7,Ra7] } (11. Qxd4 { Taking back with the queen is just as good. } 11... Bf6 { Level: the pawn is back either way, though his bishop now hits your queen. [%cal Rf6d4] }) (11. c3 { The c pawn hits d4, but he can take first. } 11... dxc3 12. Nxc3 { About level, but he traded his pawn for yours, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rc3] }) (11. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it gives his c pawn time to step up. } 11... c5 { Slightly better for him: his c pawn now guards d4, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (11. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but that gives his c pawn time too. } 11... c5 { Better for him: the pawn on d4 is guarded now, and he stays a pawn up. [%csl Rd4] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it walks into his d pawn. } 11... dxc3 { Lost for you: his d pawn took your knight. [%csl Rc3] }) (11. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, where the pawn on d4 can take it. } 11... dxe3 { Lost for you: the pawn took your bishop instead of falling itself. [%csl Re3] }) (11. Bg5 { The bishop comes out to g5, but his bishop can take it with his queen behind. } 11... Bxg5 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, and his queen now guards g5 as well. [%cal Rd8g5] }) 1-0


