{ The Scotch gambit main line: your knight has just won the pawn back on d4, and your bishop pins his knight on c6. }
7... Bc5 { His bishop comes to c5 and hits your knight on d4, which only your queen guards. How do you keep it safe? [%cal Rc5d4] } 8. Be3 { The bishop guards your knight on d4. If he trades his bishop for it, take back with the queen. [%cal Ge3d4] } (8. O-O { Castling is just as good: your queen still guards the knight. } 8... O-O { Level: both kings are safe, and your knight on d4 holds. [%csl Gd4] }) (8. Nxc6 { Taking on c6 at once is fine too, but it gives his bishop a shot at f2. } 8... Bxf2+ 9. Kf1 bxc6 { Level, but his bishop took f2 with check, and your king has lost the right to castle. [%csl Rf1] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out to trade off his knight on e4. } 8... Nxc3 9. bxc3 { Level: one pair of knights came off, but your c pawns are doubled. [%csl Rc3] }) (8. Bxc6+ { Taking now is a move too early: he takes back and never has to spend a move breaking the pin. } 8... bxc6 { About level: his bishops now have open roads, and your knight on d4 is still hit. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (8. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, but it is slow. } 8... O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (8. Nf3 { The knight steps away, but now his bishop sees f2. } 8... Nxf2 { Lost for you: his knight took the pawn on f2 and forks your queen and rook. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (8. Nb3 { The knight steps back to b3, but it lets his queen and bishop aim at f2. } 8... Qh4 { Lost for you: his queen and bishop both hit f2, and your king is in danger. [%cal Rh4f2,Rc5f2] }) (8. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but it cuts your queen off from d4. } 8... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight, and nothing can take back. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out to f4, and your knight on d4 keeps only one guard. } 8... O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, and your knight still needs care. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but nothing guards it. } 8... Qxg5 { Lost for you: his queen took your bishop for free. [%csl Rg5] }) 8... Bd7 { His bishop breaks the pin; castling was his other good move. His pieces are free now. What is he threatening? } 9. Bxc6 { Bishop takes knight before it can jump, and his pawns get bent. Had he castled, taking on c6 would have been right as well. [%cal Gb5c6] } (9. Nxc6 { Taking with the knight trades off your good knight instead. } 9... bxc6 10. Bxc5 Nxc5 { Slightly better for him: your bishop on b5 is attacked, and his knight sits well on c5. [%cal Rc6b5] }) (9. Nf3 { The knight steps back, out of the bishop's aim. } 9... Bxe3 10. fxe3 { Slightly better for him: the dark bishops came off, and your e pawns are doubled. [%csl Re3] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it can be traded off at once. } 9... Nxc3 10. bxc3 Nxe5 { Slightly better for him: one pair of knights came off, and his other knight grabbed your e pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (9. O-O { Castling first lets his knight jump right away. } 9... Nxe5 { Better for him: his knight grabbed your e pawn, and his bishop on d7 now hits yours on b5. [%cal Rd7b5] }) (9. c3 { The c pawn guards your knight, but his knight gets to jump first. } 9... Nxe5 { Better for him: his knight grabbed your e pawn, and his bishop now hits yours on b5. [%cal Rd7b5] }) (9. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but it blocks your queen from guarding d4. [%cal Rd1d4] } 9... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took yours on d4, and taking back only loses your bishop to his. [%csl Rd4] }) 9... bxc6 { He takes back with the b pawn, so his c pawns are doubled. What now? } 10. Nd2 { Your knight challenges his knight on e4. If he castles or makes a queen move instead, take on e4. [%cal Gd2e4] } (10. O-O { Castling is natural, and just as good. } 10... Bb6 { Just as good: your king is safe, and his knight on e4 can still be challenged. [%csl Gg1] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his bishop can pin it. } 10... Bb4 { About level: his bishop pins your knight, and his knight on e4 stays strong. [%cal Rb4e1] }) (10. Qd3 { The queen steps up to hit his knight, but his d pawn guards it. } 10... Qe7 { About level: your queen stands on d3, but his queen now attacks your pawn on e5. [%cal Re7e5] }) (10. c3 { The c pawn is solid, but it is slow. } 10... Qe7 { About level, but your pawn on e5 needs help against his queen, and you have not castled. [%csl Re5] }) (10. f3 { The f pawn kicks the knight, but your king has not castled yet. } 10... Qh4+ { Slightly better for him: his queen checks, and your king is stuck in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) 10... Nxd2 { He trades knights on d2. Take back the right way. } 11. Qxd2 { That is the Scotch gambit, calm and clear: the pawn came back long ago, his best knight is gone, and his c pawns are doubled. You can castle either side. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] } (11. Kxd2 { Taking with the king keeps your queen home, but your king loses castling. } 11... O-O { Better for him: your king stands in the middle for good. [%csl Rd2] }) (11. Bxd2 { Taking back with the bishop leaves your knight on d4 unguarded. } 11... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) 1-0


