{ The Scotch gambit, with your bishop just arrived on c4. Remember the rule for when his bishop comes out. }
4... Bc5 { His bishop comes to c5 and guards the pawn on d4. How do you go on? } 5. c3 { Your c pawn joins the gambit. If he takes it, a trick on f7 is waiting; if his pawn pushes on to d3 instead, just castle. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Ng5 { The knight jumps at f7, a wild try that is just as good. } 5... Nh6 { Level: that road leads to a knight sacrifice on f7, a study of its own. [%csl Rf7] }) (5. O-O { Castling is natural, but it gives his pawn time to settle. } 5... d6 6. c3 Bg4 { About level: his pieces come out with a pin, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (5. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but he simply blocks with a knight. } 5... Nge7 { About level: he blocks calmly and keeps his pawn on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (5. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it blocks your queen from d4. } 5... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight hits your pawn, and his pawn on d4 is safe. [%csl Rd4] }) (5. Nc3 { Developing the knight hands it to the pawn on d4. } 5... dxc3 6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 still wins his bishop back. } 6... Kxf7 7. Qd5+ Kf8 8. Qxc5+ { Much better for him: you won his bishop back, but you are still a knight down. [%csl Rc3] }) (5. Nxd4 { Taking back the pawn runs into his bishop and knight. } 5... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight, and his knight on c6 guards d4, so taking back loses your queen. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (5. Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn, but two of his pieces hit d4. } 5... Nxd4 6. Nxd4 { Lost for you: your queen went for a knight, and his bishop is already aiming at your knight on d4. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (5. e5 { The pawn push comes before your c pawn joins the gambit. } 5... Nge7 { Slightly better for him: his knight comes out, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%csl Rd4] }) (5. Bf4 { The bishop comes out to f4, but it does nothing about the pawn on d4. } 5... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight hits your e pawn, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (5. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 comes too early: his king takes the bishop. } 5... Kxf7 { Much better for him: a bishop for a pawn, and his king is safe enough. [%csl Rf7] }) (5. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3 to block his bishop, but the pawn on d4 can take it. } 5... dxe3 { Much better for him: his pawn took your bishop. [%csl Re3] }) 5... Nf6 { His knight hits your king pawn; had his pawn gone to d6 instead, your c pawn would take on d4. How do you answer? } 6. e5 { Your pawn kicks the knight. If it jumps to e4 instead, your bishop to d5 hits it. [%cal Ge5f6] } (6. cxd4 { Taking on d4 at once is just as good: that road leads to the Italian main line. } 6... Bb4+ { Level: his bishop checks, and you are on another opening's main road. [%csl Bb4] }) (6. O-O { Castling first lets him grab your e pawn. } 6... Nxe4 { About level, but he took your e pawn, and you are two pawns down for now. [%csl Re4] }) (6. b4 { The b pawn chases his bishop, but it loosens your queen side. } 6... Bb6 { About level: his bishop steps back to b6, and your pawn move gained nothing. [%csl Rb4] }) (6. Bb3 { Stepping the bishop back lets him grab your e pawn. } 6... Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: he took your e pawn for free, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Re4] }) (6. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn with the knight forgets about your e pawn. } 6... Nxe4 { Better for him: his knight took your e pawn, and his bishop hits your knight on d4. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen guards your pawn, but it is a slow move. } 6... O-O { Better for him: he castled, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it gives his pawn time to take on c3. } 6... dxc3 { Better for him: his pawn took on c3, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight, but his h pawn kicks it. } 6... h6 { Better for him: his pawn chases your bishop, and the pawn on d4 still stands. [%cal Rh6g5] }) (6. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, into the reach of his pawn. } 6... dxe3 { Lost for you: his pawn took your bishop, a whole piece. [%csl Re3] }) (6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 gives up your bishop for a pawn. } 6... Kxf7 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and his knight still hits your e pawn. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (6. Qxd4 { The queen takes on d4, but his bishop guards the pawn. [%cal Rc5d4] } 6... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen. [%csl Rd4] }) 6... d5 { He hits your bishop with the d pawn; against a knight jump to g4 or a queen move, your c pawn would take on d4. Where does your bishop go now? [%cal Rd5c4] } 7. Bb5 { The bishop pins his knight on c6 to his king, and his other knight is still attacked, so it must move. [%cal Gb5e8,Ge5f6] } (7. Be2 { Stepping back to e2 is just as good. } 7... Ne4 { Level: his knight jumps to e4, and the pawn on d4 is still there for your c pawn. [%cal Gc3d4] }) (7. Bb3 { Back to b3 is a little slow. } 7... Ne4 { About level: his knight jumps to e4, and your bishop on b3 hits only a pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (7. Bd3 { Back to d3 blocks your own queen. } 7... Ne4 { About level: his knight lands on e4, and your bishop stands in the way. [%csl Rd3] }) (7. exf6 { Taking the knight lets his pawn take your bishop. } 7... dxc4 8. fxg7 Rg8 { Slightly better for him: the pieces came off even, and his bishops and pawns rule the middle. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. cxd4 { Taking on d4 first leaves your bishop hanging. } 7... dxc4 8. exf6 Bb4+ { Slightly better for him: the pieces came off even, but your pawn on f6 has no guard, and his bishops are free. [%cal Rb4e1] }) (7. Bxd5 { The bishop takes a pawn, but his knight guards d5. [%cal Rf6d5] } 7... Nxd5 { Lost for you: you gave a bishop for one pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) (7. O-O { Castling ignores the pawn that hits your bishop. } 7... dxc4 8. exf6 Qxf6 { Better for him: bishop for knight, but you are two pawns down. [%csl Rf6] }) (7. exd6 { Taking en passant removes his d pawn, but it opens the game for his queen. } 7... Qxd6 { Better for him: his queen took back, and his pieces come out fast. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. Nxd4 { The knight takes the pawn on d4, but your bishop and e pawn are still hit. } 7... Nxe5 { Better for him: his knight grabbed your e pawn, and your bishop on c4 is still attacked. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (7. Qxd4 { The queen takes on d4, forgetting his bishop on c5. } 7... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop swallowed your queen. [%csl Rd4] }) 7... Ne4 { The knight hops to e4, out of reach of your pawns. What now? } 8. cxd4 { The gambit pawn is back, and your pawns on d4 and e5 now stand side by side. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (8. Bxc6+ { Taking the knight with check first is about as good. } 8... bxc6 9. cxd4 { Level: the pawn came back, though he now has two bishops. [%csl Gd4] }) (8. O-O { Castling first lets his pawn escape the trade. } 8... dxc3 9. Nxc3 Nxc3 10. bxc3 { About level, but your big middle is gone: his pawn from d4 traded itself off, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rc3] }) (8. Nxd4 { The knight takes the pawn, and your c pawn is left with nothing to do. } 8... O-O { Slightly better for him: you have no pawn middle, and his knight on e4 is strong. [%csl Re4] }) (8. b4 { The b pawn kicks his bishop, but your centre never forms. } 8... Bb6 { Slightly better for him: his bishop steps back safely, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it forgets the pawn on f2. } 8... Nxf2 { Much better for him: his knight took on f2 and hits your queen and rook. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but only your king guards the pawn on f2. } 8... Nxf2 { Much better for him: his knight grabbed that pawn and forks your queen and rook. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, into his pawn's reach. } 8... dxe3 { Lost for you: the pawn took your bishop, and your c pawn never took on d4. [%csl Re3] }) (8. Qxd4 { The queen takes on d4, but his bishop is waiting. } 8... Bxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen on d4. }) 8... Bb4+ { He checks with the bishop; stepping back to b6 was his other try, and against it you would just castle. How do you meet the check? } 9. Bd2 { The bishop blocks and offers a trade. He can take on d2 with his knight or with his bishop. [%csl Gd2] } (9. Nbd2 { Blocking with the knight is just as good. } 9... O-O { Level: your pawns on d4 and e5 still stand, and his king is castled. [%csl Gd4] }) (9. Nfd2 { Blocking with the other knight is also playable. } 9... O-O { About level, but your knight left f3, where it guarded d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (9. Kf1 { Stepping aside with the king costs you castling. } 9... O-O { Slightly better for him: your king can never castle now. [%csl Rf1] }) (9. Nc3 { Blocking with the knight on c3 lets his knight take it. } 9... Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxc3+ { Better for him: his bishop took a pawn with check, and your rook in the corner is next. [%cal Rc3a1] }) 9... Nxd2 { He takes with the knight. Take back, and choose your piece with care. } 10. Nbxd2 { Your queen knight takes back and comes out at once, as it would after his bishop took. The Scotch gambit with the c pawn: the pawn is back, and your pawns on d4 and e5 own the middle. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (10. Bxc6+ { Taking on c6 with check first is just as good. } 10... bxc6 11. Nbxd2 { Level: his c pawns are doubled, and your knight still took back. [%csl Rc6] }) (10. Nfxd2 { Taking back with the other knight gives up your guard of d4. } 10... O-O { Slightly better for him: your knight left f3, and your pawn on d4 is weaker. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Qxd2 { Taking back with the queen walks into his bishop on b4. } 10... Bxd2+ 11. Nbxd2 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen, and you only got the bishop. [%csl Rd2] }) 1-0


