ightly better for him: his bishop steps to b6 to meet yours, and he keeps the pawn. [%cal Rb6e3] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The bishop takes on f7 with check, but it is lost. } 7... Kxf7 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and you are a whole piece down. [%csl Rf7] }) 7... d6 { His d pawn steps up; stepping his bishop back to b6 was a little better. Look at the pawns in front of his king: which one is weak? } 8. Qb3 { Your queen and bishop both aim at f7, and the queen hits b7 too. [%cal Gb3f7,Gb3b7] } (8. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, a quiet move. } 8... Nh6 { About level: his knight heads for g4, and your queen never reached f7. [%csl Rh6] }) (8. Re1 { The rook guards your e pawn. } 8... Be6 { About level: his bishop on e6 blocks your bishop's view of f7. [%csl Re6] }) (8. Ba3 { The bishop hits his d pawn. } 8... Nh6 { About level: his knight guards f7 from h6, and your bishop hits a guarded pawn. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (8. Ng5 { The knight hops to g5 toward f7, but he covers it. } 8... Nh6 { Slightly better for him: his knight covers f7, and yours on g5 has to go back. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (8. e5 { The pawn push hits his d pawn, but he develops calmly. } 8... Nge7 { Slightly better for him: his king side is covered, and your pawn on e5 can be won. [%csl Re5] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but it can be blocked. } 8... Nge7 { Slightly better for him: his knight blocks, and your bishop achieves nothing. [%csl Rg5] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out and eyes his d pawn. } 8... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight comes out, and your queen never reached f7. [%csl Rf4] }) (8. Bb2 { The bishop comes out to b2, where your c pawn blocks it. } 8... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight comes out in time, and your bishop bites on c3. [%csl Rb2] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop comes out to e3, a calm move. } 8... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight comes out, and the chance against f7 is gone. [%csl Rf7] }) (8. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 costs you the bishop. } 8... Kxf7 