ight took your bishop. [%csl Rh6] }) (5. Bg5 { The bishop comes out to g5, where his h pawn takes it. } 5... hxg5 { Lost for you: his h pawn took your bishop. [%csl Rg5] }) 5... Nxd4 { He trades knights, a small mistake: developing his other knight was better. His bishop to c5 would lose his f7 pawn to a bishop check. } 6. Qxd4 { Your queen takes back and stands in the middle. Against his knight or queen to f6, push your e pawn. [%csl Gd4] } (6. Nc3 { Developing first forgets the knight on d4. } 6... Nc6 { Much better for him: his knight escaped, and you are a whole piece down. [%csl Rc6] }) (6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 costs your bishop for a pawn. } 6... Kxf7 7. Qxd4 { Much better for him: you got the knight back, but your bishop is gone for one pawn. [%csl Rf7] }) 6... d6 { His d pawn steps up, a sound move; the pawn to c5 would have left a hole on d5. Bring a piece out. } 7. Nc3 { The knight comes out and guards your e pawn. His bishop to e6 is his other plan; against his queen to f6, avoid the trade by stepping back. [%cal Gc3e4] } (7. O-O { Castling is just as good. } 7... Ne7 { Slightly better for you: your king is safe, and your lead stays. [%csl Gg1] }) (7. Be2 { The bishop steps back to e2, nearly as good, though it had no reason to. } 7... Nf6 { Slightly better for you, but the bishop left its best diagonal. [%csl Re2] }) (7. Bf4 { The bishop comes out to f4, a fine square. } 7... Ne7 { Slightly better for you: a good move, just not the most direct. [%csl Gf4] }) (7. h4 { The h pawn steps up to keep his g pawn back, an odd move that is almost as good. } 7... Ne7 { Slightly better for you: his g pawn cannot safely step to g5, and your lead stays. [%cal Gh4g5] }) (7. Be3 { The bishop comes to e3, a little passive. } 7... Nf6 { About level: he develops calmly, and your lead shrinks. [%csl Re3] }) (7. e5 { Pushing the e pawn now only hits his guarded pawn on d6. } 7... Qe7 { Level: his queen pins your pawn to your king, and your lead is gone. [%cal Re7e1] }) (