s behind his bishop. } 5... Bxg5 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, and taking back with the knight drops it to his queen. [%cal Rd8g5] }) 5... Nxd4 { He trades knights; had his knight jumped to e5, your bishop would step back to b3. Take back. } 6. Qxd4 { Your queen takes back and rules the middle. If his bishop hits her from f6, push your e pawn at it. [%csl Gd4] } (6. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 costs a bishop for one pawn. } 6... Kxf7 7. Qxd4 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and one pawn is poor payment. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his knight on d4 still needs taking. } 6... Bf6 { Much better for him: he guarded his knight, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rd4] }) 6... Nf6 { His knight comes out and hits your e pawn. How do you answer? } 7. e5 { The pawn kicks the knight, and no knight move helps: his best is to hit back in the middle. If his pawn comes to c5 instead, step your queen back, and the knight still falls. [%cal Ge5f6] } (7. Nc3 { Developing the knight is just as good. } 7... c6 { About level: a calm game where you keep the better pieces. [%csl Gc3] }) (7. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, fine too. } 7... O-O { About level: he castles, and the game stays calm. [%csl Gf4] }) (7. O-O { Castling is fine too. } 7... O-O { About level: both kings are safe, and your queen still rules the middle. [%csl Gd4] }) (7. Bb3 { Stepping the bishop back to b3 first is just as good. } 7... d6 { About level: his d pawn steps up, and your bishop is safe on b3. [%csl Gb3] }) (7. a4 { The a pawn steps up, an odd move that is fine too. } 7... d6 { About level: his d pawn steps up, and your a pawn has done little. [%csl Ra4] }) (7. Bg5 { The bishop hits his knight, a calmer try. } 7... O-O { Level: he castles, and your lead is small. [%csl Rg5] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 grabs a pawn, but not for free. } 7... Kxf7 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and a single pawn is poor pay. [%csl Rf7] }) 7... d5 { His d pawn hits your bishop, his only good defence: had his knight gone back to g8, your queen to d5 would threaten mate. Your bishop and his knight are both attacked. } 8. exf6 { Your pawn takes the knight, and his pawn will take your bishop. If his bishop takes back on f6 instead, your queen takes the pawn on d5. [%csl Gf6] } (8. Bb3 { Stepping back to b3 is also good. } 8... Nd7 9. Qxd5 { Level: you won the pawn on d5, though his pieces come out fast. [%csl Gd5] }) (8. Bd3 { Back to d3, a quieter choice. } 8... Nd7 { Level: his knight steps back, and your pawn on e5 holds for now. [%csl Ge5] }) (8. Bb5+ { The bishop checks, but a knight blocks. } 8... Nd7 { Level: he blocks with the knight, and your bishop stands out of play on b5. [%csl Rb5] }) (8. exd6 { Taking en passant opens the game, but it trades off your strong pawn. } 8... Qxd6 9. Qxd6 Bxd6 { Level: the queens came off, and so did your strong e pawn. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Be2 { Stepping back to e2 is safe but slow. } 8... Ne4 { Level: his knight jumps to e4, and the chance is gone. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Bxd5 { Grabbing the pawn on d5 gives up your bishop: his knight and queen both guard it. [%cal Rf6d5,Rd8d5] } 8... Qxd5 { Lost for you: his queen took your bishop, and one pawn is all you got. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. Qxd5 { The queen grabs the pawn on d5, but his knight guards it. } 8... Nxd5 { Lost for you: the knight from f6 took your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) 8... dxc4 { His pawn takes your bishop, and now his queen faces yours down the d column. } 9. Qxd8+ { Queen for queen with check, and his bishop has to take back. [%cal Gd4d8] } (9. Qxc4 { Taking the pawn back leaves your pawn on f6 alone. } 9... Bxf6 { About level: his bishop took your pawn on f6, and material is level again. [%csl Rf6] }) (9. Qe4 { The queen steps aside and keeps the queens on. } 9... gxf6 { Level: his g pawn took yours, and his king side is split, but you are a pawn down for now. [%csl Rf6] }) (9. Qe3 { The queen steps back to e3. } 9... gxf6 { About level: the queens stay on, and his pawn took yours on f6. [%csl Rf6] }) (9. fxe7 { Your pawn takes his bishop, but your queen is hanging. } 9... Qxd4 { Lost for you: his queen took yours on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (9. fxg7 { Your pawn grabs on g7, but your queen is left hanging. } 9... Qxd4 { Lost for you: his queen took yours, and the pawn on g7 cannot make up for it. [%csl Rd4] }) 9... Bxd8 { He takes back with the bishop. Is anything of his still loose? } 10. fxg7 { Your pawn takes on g7 and hits his rook on h8. [%cal Gg7h8] } (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but your pawn on f6 is left hanging. } 10... Bxf6 { Slightly better for him: his bishop took your pawn on f6, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Rf6] }) (10. Bg5 { The bishop guards your pawn on f6, a slower idea. } 10... Rg8 { About level: his rook covers g7, and your pawn on f6 can no longer grab it. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (10. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it guards nothing. } 10... Bxf6 { About level: his bishop took your pawn, and his bishops are strong. [%csl Rf6] }) (10. O-O { Castling is natural, but your pawn on f6 is left hanging. } 10... Bxf6 { Better for him: his bishop took your pawn on f6, and it eyes your b2 pawn next. [%cal Rf6b2] }) (10. Be3 { The bishop comes out to e3, and the pawn on f6 is left alone. } 10... Bxf6 { Slightly better for him: his bishop took your pawn, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Rf6] }) 10... Rg8 { His rook steps to g8 and hits your pawn. What now? } 11. Bh6 { The bishop guards your pawn on g7 from h6. [%cal Gh6g7] } (11. O-O { Castling first lets his rook take the pawn. } 11... Rxg7 { Level: his rook took your pawn on g7, and material is level. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it leaves your pawn on g7 alone. } 11... Rxg7 { Level: his rook took your pawn, and your bishop on f4 just watches. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on g7 has no guard. } 11... Rxg7 { Level: the pawn on g7 fell, and the extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. Be3 { The bishop comes out, but your pawn on g7 has no guard. } 11... Rxg7 { Level: his rook took on g7, and your extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but your pawn on g7 is left alone. } 11... Be6 { Level: he develops first, and the pawn on g7 falls next. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. c3 { The c pawn steps up, but your pawn on g7 needs help. } 11... Rxg7 { Level: his rook won your pawn on g7 back. [%csl Rg7] }) (11. Bg5 { The bishop comes out to g5, but his bishop can take it. } 11... Bxg5 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, and nothing can take back. [%csl Rg5] }) 11... Bf6 { His bishop joins the attack on your pawn, and it also hits b2. } 12. Nc3 { Your knight shuts his bishop off from b2. The queens are off and you are a pawn up for now, though he hits g7 twice, and his c pawns stay doubled. [%csl Gg7,Rc4,Rc7] } (12. c3 { The c pawn shuts the bishop off too, but your knight stays at home. } 12... Bxg7 13. Bxg7 Rxg7 { Level: the dark bishops came off on g7, and his rook took your pawn there. [%csl Rg7] }) (12. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but it leaves b2 unguarded. } 12... Bxb2 { Better for him: his bishop grabbed the pawn on b2 and eyes your rook on a1. [%cal Rb2a1] }) (12. Bd2 { The bishop steps back, but your b2 pawn falls. } 12... Bxb2 { Better for him: his bishop grabbed your b2 pawn and now hits your rook in the corner. [%csl Rb2] }) (12. O-O { Castling is natural, but your b pawn is left alone. } 12... Bxb2 { Much better for him: his bishop took on b2, and your rook on a1 is next. [%cal Rb2a1] }) 1-0


