{ Now the Scotch gambit traps. You have just castled against his knight on g4, and your pawn on e5 looks loose. }
6... Ngxe5 { He grabs your pawn with a knight, and it looks free. It is not: his bishop to e7 was the right move. Can you prove it? } 7. Nxe5 { You trade knights, and now his other knight is the only piece that can take back. [%cal Gf3e5] } (7. Re1 { The rook pins at once, but he simply guards the knight. } 7... d6 { Slightly better for him: his d pawn and other knight both guard e5, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Bb3 { The bishop steps back, but his knight trades on f3. } 7... Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3 { Better for him: one pair of knights came off, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Rf3] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen pins the knight, but it can be guarded. } 7... d6 { Better for him: his d pawn guards the knight, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Nxd4 { The knight takes the pawn on d4, but your bishop is attacked. } 7... Nxc4 8. Qe2+ Be7 9. Qxc4 { Better for him: your queen won the piece back with a check, but he is still a pawn up. [%csl Gc4] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but his knight takes it. } 7... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and he is a piece and a pawn up. [%csl Rf7] }) 7... Nxe5 { His knight takes back on e5, right in front of his own king. } 8. Re1 { The rook pins his knight on e5 to his king; blocking on e7 would leave that knight with no guard. [%cal Ge1e8] } (8. Qxd4 { The queen grabs the gambit pawn back, but that lets his knight off the hook. } 8... d6 { Level: the pawn is back, but his knight on e5 is no longer in trouble. [%csl Re5] }) (8. Bb3 { The bishop steps back, and he gets time to block the e column. } 8... Be7 { Slightly better for him: his bishop blocks the e column, and your chance is gone. [%csl Re7] }) (8. Qe2 { The queen pins the knight, but his queen blocks the pin. } 8... Qe7 { Better for him: his queen blocks, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Re7] }) (8. Bb5 { The bishop goes to b5, but a pawn chases it. } 8... c6 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chases your bishop, and the pin never came. [%csl Rc6] }) (8. f4 { The pawn kicks the knight, but nothing pins it yet. } 8... Nxc4 { Much better for him: his knight got away by taking your bishop. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bxf7+ { The bishop takes on f7 with check instead of pinning. } 8... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took the bishop, and the pin never came. [%csl Rf7] }) 8... d6 { He guards the knight with his d pawn; his queen to e7 was the tougher defence. His bishop to d6 or his f pawn would guard it too, and the knight would still be pinned. } 9. f4 { Your f pawn hits the pinned knight, and it cannot run. If his bishop hits your queen from g4, take it with her: the knight cannot take back. [%cal Gf4e5] } (9. Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn, but the pinned knight gets help. } 9... Be7 { Slightly better for him: the bishop steps in between, and the knight is free again. [%csl Re7] }) (9. Bf4 { The bishop attacks the knight too, but it can be guarded. } 9... Be7 { Slightly better for him: his bishop breaks the pin, and the knight is safe. [%csl Re7] }) (9. Qh5 { The queen hits the knight and f7, but he defends both. } 9... Qe7 { Better for him: his queen breaks the pin and guards everything. [%csl Re7] }) (9. Bb5+ { The bishop checks, but a pawn blocks, and the pin idea fades. } 9... c6 { Better for him: the pawn on c6 blocks the check and chases your bishop away. [%cal Rc6b5] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out to c3, but his d pawn can take it. } 9... dxc3 { Better for him: his pawn took your knight. [%csl Rc3] }) (9. Rxe5+ { The rook takes the knight at once, but his d pawn takes back. } 9... dxe5 { Much better for him: his pawn took your rook, and you got only a knight. [%csl Re5] }) (9. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but it is just lost. } 9... Kxf7 { Much better for him: his king took your bishop, and his knight is free again. [%csl Rf7] }) (9. Be3 { The bishop steps to e3, where his pawn can take it. } 9... dxe3 { Lost for you: his d pawn took your bishop. [%csl Re3] }) 9... Be7 { He brings his bishop out, and that unpins his knight: now it hits your bishop on c4. What comes first? } 10. fxe5 { Your pawn takes the knight: a whole piece won. Now watch. [%tail] } (10. Bb5+ { The bishop checks, but his bishop on e7 already broke the pin, so the knight simply blocks. } 10... Nd7 { Better for him: the knight escaped by blocking the check, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rd7] }) (10. Bb3 { The bishop steps back instead of taking. } 10... Nc6 { Better for him: the knight walked away, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rc6] }) (10. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn on d4, but his knight takes your bishop. } 10... Nxc4 11. Qxc4 { Better for him: your queen took back, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Gc4] }) (10. Rxe5 { The rook grabs the knight, but his d pawn guards it. } 10... dxe5 { Lost for you: his pawn took your rook, and a knight is poor change for it. [%csl Re5] }) (10. Bxf7+ { The check on f7 lets the knight off the hook. } 10... Nxf7 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop and got away. [%csl Rf7] }) 10... dxe5 11. Rxe5 { A knight for two pawns, and your rook owns the open e column. That grab on e5 cost him a whole piece. [%csl Ge5] } 1-0


