aped to e5, and nothing was won. [%csl Re5] }) (10. Bb3 { The bishop steps back, and the knight jumps away too. } 10... Nge5 { About level: his knight found e5, and the chance is gone. [%csl Re5] }) (10. Nxd4 { The knight takes the pawn on d4, and your king is left open. } 10... Bh2+ { Lost for you: his bishop checks, and his queen will take your knight. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (10. Qxd4 { The queen takes on d4, forgetting his knight on c6. } 10... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen, and his other knight is still alive. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Bxf7+ { The bishop checks on f7, but his rook takes it. } 10... Rxf7 { Much better for him: his rook took the bishop, and his knight got time to escape. [%csl Rf7] }) 10... Bxg4 { His bishop takes back on g4 and pins your knight on f3 to your queen. [%cal Rg4d1] } 11. Nbd2 { Your other knight guards the pinned one. A knight for two pawns: your capture on d6 left two of his pieces hanging at once. That is a Scotch gambit trap. [%cal Gd2f3] } (11. Be2 { The bishop breaks the pin at once, also good. } 11... Qf6 { Better for you: still a knight for two pawns, though his queen joins the attack on f3. [%cal Rf6f3] }) (11. Re4 { The rook steps up to hit his bishop. } 11... Ne5 { Better for you, but his knight on e5 now hits your pinned knight too. [%cal Re5f3] }) (11. Bb5 { The bishop hits his knight in turn, a good try. } 11... Qd7 { Better for you: still a piece up for two pawns, though his bishop still pins your knight. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (11. Bd3 { The bishop steps back to d3. } 11... Ne5 { Better for you: you keep the extra piece, though his knight comes to e5 to hit f3. [%csl Re5] }) (11. Nxd4 { The knight takes the pawn on d4, but it was pinned to your queen. } 11... Bxd1 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen on d1. [%csl Rd1] }) (11. Qxd4 { The queen takes the pawn on d4, but his knight guards it. } 11... Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen on d4, a whole queen for a pawn. }) (11. Nc3 { The knight comes out to c