{ Your c pawn has just joined the Scotch gambit against his bishop on c5. }
5... dxc3 { He grabs a second pawn, and it looks free. It is a mistake: his knight to f6 was the move. Where is the trick? } 6. Bxf7+ { Check! Your bishop gives itself up to drag his king out. If his king steps to f8 instead, your bishop just steps back to b3. [%cal Gc4f7] } (6. Nxc3 { Taking back with the knight is natural, but it misses the trick. } 6... d6 { Level: a normal gambit, a pawn down, but the trick on f7 was there. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. O-O { Castling ignores the pawn. } 6... d6 { Level: you are two pawns down for fast development. The trick on f7 was stronger. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. Qb3 { The queen hits f7 at once, but his queen guards it. } 6... Qe7 { Level: his queen guards f7, and the chance is gone. [%cal Re7f7] }) (6. bxc3 { Taking back with the b pawn splits your pawns. } 6... d6 { Better for him: your pawns are split, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. e5 { The pawn push is too slow: his pawn on c3 is still there. } 6... d5 { Better for him: his d pawn hits your bishop, and you are two pawns down. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but his pawn can take it with check. } 6... cxd2+ { Lost for you: his pawn took your knight with check. [%csl Rd2] }) (6. Be3 { The bishop blocks his bishop, but your b pawn falls. } 6... cxb2 { Lost for you: his pawn took on b2, and your rook in the corner is next. [%cal Rb2a1] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but his pawn takes on b2. } 6... cxb2 { Lost for you: his pawn took on b2 and hits your rook, one step from a new queen. [%cal Rb2a1] }) 6... Kxf7 { His king takes the bishop, and now it stands in the open. } 7. Qd5+ { Check, and your queen also hits his bishop on c5: a fork. [%cal Gd5f7,Gd5c5] } (7. Qb3+ { A check from b3, but a pawn blocks it. } 7... d5 { Much better for him: his d pawn blocks, and you are a bishop and a pawn down. [%csl Rd5] }) (7. Ng5+ { The knight checks, but his king simply steps away. } 7... Kf8 { Much better for him: your knight check leads nowhere, and you are a bishop and a pawn down. [%csl Rf8] }) (7. Nxc3 { Taking the pawn back forgets why you gave the bishop. } 7... d6 { Much better for him: you gave a bishop and have only pawns to show for it. [%csl Rc3] }) (7. O-O { Castling is natural, but it lets his king escape the checks. } 7... d6 { Much better for him: his d pawn guards his bishop, and you are a bishop and a pawn down. [%csl Rc5] }) (7. bxc3 { Taking back the pawn is natural, but it lets his king breathe. } 7... Nf6 { Lost for you: his knight comes out, and you are a whole bishop down. [%csl Rc3] }) 7... Ke8 { His king steps back to e8; on f8 your queen would take the bishop with check. } 8. Qh5+ { Another check first: his king must move or a pawn must block, and his bishop on c5 still hangs. [%cal Gh5e8] } (8. Qxc5 { Taking the bishop at once is just as good. } 8... Qe7 { About level: you won the bishop back, and his queen offers a trade. [%csl Gc5] }) (8. Nxc3 { Grabbing the pawn first gives him time to guard his bishop. } 8... d6 { Much better for him: the fork is gone, and you are a bishop down. [%csl Rc5] }) (8. bxc3 { Taking the pawn back lets his bishop escape. } 8... Qe7 { Lost for you: his queen covers the bishop, and you are a bishop down. [%csl Rc5] }) 8... g6 { He blocks with the g pawn, which leaves holes around his king; his king to f8 was better. What now? } 9. Qxc5 { Your queen takes the bishop: the fork is cashed. [%csl Gc5] } (9. Qd5 { The queen goes back to d5, but with no check his d pawn can guard the bishop. } 9... d6 { Much better for him: his pawn guards the bishop now, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rd6] }) 9... d6 { His pawn hits your queen; taking on b2 was his other try, and his queen to e7 would have been worse. Where does she go? } 10. Qxc3 { The queen steps away and takes back the pawn on c3. Material is level, his king can never castle, and his king side is full of holes. [%csl Gc3] } (10. Qe3 { Stepping back to e3 is just as good. } 10... cxb2 11. Bxb2 { Better for you: you are a pawn down, but his king can never castle and stands exposed. [%csl Gb2] }) (10. Qa3 { The queen steps to a3, about as good. } 10... Bg4 { Better for you, though his bishop now hits your knight on f3. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (10. Qc4 { The queen steps to c4, but his pawn on c3 is left alive. } 10... Bg4 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn on c3 is still alive, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Rc3] }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: punish the bishop to e7"]
