[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/1bB1P3/2P2N2/P4PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/1bB1P3/2P2N2/P4PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_0_6"]

{ A Scotch gambit trap after his bishop check: your pawn on c3 has just taken back and hit his bishop on b4. }
6... Be7 { His bishop steps back to e7, a natural retreat, and a mistake: it should have gone to a5. Which of his pawns is weak now? } 7. Qd5 { Your queen and bishop both aim at f7: mate is threatened. [%cal Gd5f7,Gc4f7] } (7. Qb3 { The queen hits f7 from b3, but his knight chases her. } 7... Na5 { About level: his knight hits your queen and bishop, and the mate idea is gone. [%cal Ra5b3] }) (7. O-O { Castling is natural, but it gives him a free move. } 7... Nh6 { Level: his knight guards f7, and the chance is gone. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (7. e5 { Pushing the e pawn gains space, but it misses a chance against f7. } 7... Nh6 { Level: he develops his knight to h6, and f7 is covered. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (7. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it misses f7. } 7... Nh6 { Level: f7 is covered, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (7. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, a normal move that misses his weak pawn. } 7... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight covers his king side, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf6] }) (7. Be3 { The bishop comes out to e3, but f7 was the target. } 7... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his knight comes out, and your chance is gone. [%csl Rf6] }) (7. Ba3 { The bishop offers a trade, but it wastes your chance. } 7... Bxa3 { Slightly better for him: the dark bishops came off, and your knight takes back on the edge. [%csl Ra3] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The bishop takes on f7 with check, but it is not enough. } 7... Kxf7 { Much better for him: you are a whole piece down, and his king is safe on f7. [%csl Rf7] }) 7... Nh6 { His knight comes out just in time, his only defence against the mate. What now? } 8. Bxh6 { Your bishop takes the knight, and taking back with the g pawn lets your queen mate. If his rook comes to f8, take on g7 too. Now watch. [%cal Gd5f7][%tail] } (8. O-O { Castling lets his knight stay. } 8... O-O { Level: he castled, and his knight on h6 guards f7 for good. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (8. Qh5 { The queen hits the knight, but he castles. } 8... O-O { About level: he castled, and your queen stands in the open. [%csl Rh5] }) (8. Bb3 { Stepping the bishop back gives him time. } 8... O-O { About level: he castled, and the knight on h6 is safe. [%csl Rh6] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop comes out, but his knight stays on h6 to guard f7. [%cal Rh6f7] } 8... O-O { About level: he castled, and his knight still covers f7. [%cal Rh6f7] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, but it gives him time to castle. } 8... O-O { About level: he castled, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rg8] }) (8. e5 { The pawn push gains space, but the knight stays. } 8... d6 { Slightly better for him: his d pawn hits back, and the knight on h6 is safe. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop offers a trade, but his knight on h6 stays. } 8... Bxg5 9. Nxg5 { Better for him: the dark bishops came off, and his knight on h6 still guards f7. [%cal Rh6f7] }) 8... O-O 9. Be3 { Your bishop steps back to safety, and you are a whole knight up for a pawn. The bishop to e7 walked straight into that. [%csl Ge3] } 1-0


